---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Brand Reviews",
    tags: [
      "wheybolic protein powder",
      "best wheybolic protein",
      "gnc amp wheybolic",
      "new wheybolic"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Whey Protein Picks",
    link: "/best-whey-protein-powder/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Brand Review"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Wheybolic Protein Powder Review</Fragment>
      GNC rebuilt its flagship whey in 2026. Here is what changed between the original AMP Wheybolic and the New Wheybolic, what a scoop really costs at Amazon and at GNC, what the testing claim covers, and when ISO100 or Gold Standard is the better buy.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#short-verdict">The Short Verdict</a>
      <a href="#old-vs-new">Original vs New Wheybolic</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">Our Picks</a>
      <a href="#label">What Is in New Wheybolic</a>
      <a href="#scoops">Scoops and Servings</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#testing">Testing: Informed Choice</a>
      <a href="#flavors">Flavors and Taste</a>
      <a href="#lineup">Original, Ripped or Alpha 2.0</a>
      <a href="#rivals">Wheybolic vs ISO100 vs ON</a>
      <a href="#who">Who Should Buy It</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          The New Wheybolic is a better powder than the one it replaced: 25 g of protein per scoop instead of about 20 g, with the Velositol and enzyme blend gone and an Informed Choice certification claim on the tub. It is also an expensive one. At the Amazon prices we saw it works out to about $2.31 per 25 g of protein, so buy it at GNC on a promotion or buy a rival.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="short-verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Verdict on Wheybolic
          </h2>

          <p>
            Wheybolic is GNC's flagship whey protein, sold under its AMP (Advanced Muscle Performance) line, and it is the GNC product most people mean when they search for GNC protein. In February 2026 GNC replaced it with a reformulated version. Amazon lists it as GNC AMP New Wheybolic, and GNC's own site calls it Wheybolic Original, which is confusing, because original now means the core formula rather than the old one.
          </p>

          <p>
            The new tub is a genuine upgrade on paper. Per GNC's announcement and listings at the time of writing, one scoop now gives 25 g of protein and 3.1 g of leucine, and the full two-scoop serving gives 50 g of protein and 6.2 g of leucine. The protein comes from ultra-filtered whey isolate and whey protein. GNC dropped the Velositol and the enzyme blend from the old formula and says the new one is Informed Choice and Informed Protein certified.
          </p>

          <p>
            The problem is price. On Amazon the 26-serving tub was $119.99 when we checked, which is about $2.31 per 25 g of protein. That is more than Dymatize ISO100, a pure hydrolyzed isolate, and roughly 55% more than Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard in a 5 lb tub. At GNC's own prices, and especially during its frequent promotions, the math gets much better, so where you buy matters more than which flavor you pick.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our verdict: buy New Wheybolic at GNC when a sale brings it under about $1.50 per 25 g, use one scoop rather than two unless you are a big lifter, and check the Informed Choice registry if you are a tested athlete. If you just want good whey at the lowest cost, Gold Standard wins most days. Our <a href="/best-whey-protein-powder/">best whey protein powder</a> rankings list the other wheys that beat it on value.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE VERDICT</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">A better formula at a worse price. Buy it at GNC on sale, not on Amazon.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">New Wheybolic packs 25 g of protein into each scoop and claims Informed Choice certification. At $119.99 on Amazon it costs about $2.31 per 25 g, so Gold Standard and even ISO100 are cheaper per gram there.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            This page is a deep dive on Wheybolic alone. For the rest of GNC's range, including the bottled Wheybolic 40 shake and the older Pro Performance line, read our <a href="/gnc-protein-powder-review/">GNC protein powder review</a>. We rank on label, certification status and cost per 25 g of protein; we have not tasted these powders.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="old-vs-new" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Original AMP Wheybolic vs New Wheybolic: What Changed
          </h2>

          <p>
            The old AMP Wheybolic was built around a two-scoop serving of 40 g of protein, about 200 calories, from a blend of whey protein isolate and hydrolyzed whey. Its label added micronized L-leucine for 6.2 g of leucine per serving, 500 mg of Velositol (an amylopectin and chromium complex marketed to boost muscle protein synthesis) and a 400 mg enzyme blend of lactase and bromelain. It was sweetened with sucralose and acesulfame potassium.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm"></th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Original AMP Wheybolic</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">New Wheybolic (2026)</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protein per scoop</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 20 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protein per 2 scoops</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">40 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">50 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Calories per 2 scoops</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 200</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 240</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Leucine per 2 scoops</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">6.2 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">6.2 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protein source</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Whey isolate and hydrolyzed whey</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Ultra-filtered whey isolate and whey protein</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Velositol and enzymes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes (500 mg and 400 mg)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Removed</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Testing claim</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">LGC banned-substance testing</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Informed Choice and Informed Protein</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Tub sizes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 25 servings</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">13 and 26 servings</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The new version lists 50 g of protein in about 240 calories per two scoops, with about 5 g of carbohydrate and 2 g of sugar, according to label figures reported at launch. That is 10 g more protein for about 40 more calories, which tells you the extra weight in each scoop is mostly protein. Leucine stayed at 6.2 g per serving, so each gram of protein now carries slightly less added leucine than before.
          </p>

          <p>
            Velositol was the old formula's headline ingredient, and its removal is no loss for most buyers. The research behind it is small and mostly industry-funded, and 25 g of whey already delivers the 2-3 g of leucine that studies link to a strong muscle-building response. Losing the lactase is a slightly bigger deal if you relied on it for comfort, since the new protein source includes whey protein alongside isolate, which usually means some concentrate and some lactose.
          </p>

          <p>
            The other big change is the testing claim. The old tubs said they were tested for more than 220 banned substances through LGC skip-lot testing, LGC being the lab that runs the Informed programs. The new ones say Informed Choice certified and regularly tested for more than 285 banned substances, plus Informed Protein, which verifies protein content. Section 6 covers what that does and does not mean.
          </p>

          <p>
            Old-formula tubs have not disappeared. Amazon still listed original 40 g Wheybolic in Fruity Crisps and Chocolate Mint, sold by GNC, at $69.99 for 25 servings when we checked. That works out to about $1.75 per 25 g of protein, cheaper than the new tub on Amazon. If you do buy one, check the best-by date: a product being phased out can sit in a warehouse, and our guide to <a href="/does-protein-powder-expire/">whether protein powder expires</a> explains what an old date means.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Wheybolic Tubs Worth Considering
          </h2>

          <p>
            Two New Wheybolic sizes, the original 40 g formula that is still listed on Amazon, and two rivals for buyers who want a lower cost per gram or a pure isolate. Label figures come from GNC's and the rivals' published panels and listings at the time of writing and vary by flavor. Prices are the ones we saw on Amazon on the day and change often. We have not tasted these products.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Is in New Wheybolic
          </h2>

          <p>
            Per GNC's listings at the time of writing, a scoop of Chocolate Fudge weighs about 32.4 g and gives 25 g of protein, about 120 calories, 1 g of fat and 115 mg of sodium. Classic Vanilla is a lighter scoop at about 30.5 g, with 25 g of protein, 2 g of carbohydrate, 1 g of sugar and 3.1 g of L-leucine. The tub sizes back those scoop weights up: the 59.4 oz chocolate tub divided by its 52 scoops comes to about 32.4 g each.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
