          <p>
            That makes the new powder roughly 77% protein by weight in chocolate and about 82% in vanilla. The old formula was closer to 65%, with about 20 g of protein in a 31 g scoop. For context, a pure isolate like Dymatize ISO100 runs near 80-85%, and a mainstream blend like Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard sits around 77%. New Wheybolic is now a dense, isolate-led blend rather than the diluted 40 g two-scooper it used to be.
          </p>

          <p>
            GNC describes the protein as ultra-filtered whey isolate and whey protein. It does not say how much of each, so treat it as a blend, not a pure isolate. That matters for lactose. Isolate is filtered to remove most of it, and concentrate keeps more. If ordinary whey bloats you, a pure isolate such as ISO100 is the safer choice, and our <a href="/whey-vs-whey-isolate/">whey vs whey isolate</a> guide explains the difference in grams.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sweeteners are the one label detail we could not confirm for the new formula. The old Wheybolic used sucralose and acesulfame potassium with natural and artificial flavors, and third-party ingredient listings for the new version show the same pairing, but GNC's site blocked our attempts to read the current panel. If you avoid artificial sweeteners, check the tub before buying, or start with our <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-artificial-sweeteners/">protein powders without artificial sweeteners</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            GNC markets the new tub as gluten-free, and like every whey it contains milk. Our label database row for <a href="/protein-powder/gnc-amp-wheybolic/">GNC AMP Wheybolic Original</a> carries the per-scoop figures, flagged as low confidence until we can read a full panel, and the <a href="/brands/gnc/">GNC brand page</a> compares it with other GNC rows. If you have a milk allergy, kidney disease or are pregnant, talk to your doctor or a registered dietitian before using any high-protein supplement.
          </p>
