        <div id="scoops" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Scoops, Servings and How Much to Use
          </h2>

          <p>
            GNC's label treats two scoops as one serving, which is why the front of the tub says 50 g and the Amazon title says 25 g per scoop. Both are true. A 26-serving tub holds 52 scoops and about 1,300 g of protein, and a 13-serving tub holds 26 scoops and about 650 g. Always count the scoops, not the servings, when you compare it with brands that list one scoop as a serving.
          </p>

          <p>
            Amazon listings make this harder than it should be. Most New Wheybolic listings count two-scoop servings, but at least one, the 28.2 oz Banana Milkshake tub, was listed as 26 servings, which only works if a serving is one scoop. At about 30.7 g per scoop that tub holds roughly 26 scoops, the same as the other small tubs. Divide the net weight by about 31-32 g to get the real scoop count.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do you need 50 g in one shake? Usually not. Research on per-meal protein suggests that about 20-25 g of high-quality protein, or roughly 0.3-0.4 g per kg of body weight, captures most of the muscle-building response for most people. One scoop of New Wheybolic hits that on its own. A 2016 study by Macnaughton and colleagues found 40 g of whey beat 20 g after a whole-body workout, so big lifters and older adults can reasonably use more.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical rule is to dose by your daily target, not by the label. Many guidelines for people who train suggest about 1.6 g of protein per kg of body weight per day. Our <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">protein needs guide</a> walks through the numbers, and our guide on <a href="/when-to-take-protein-powder/">when to take protein powder</a> explains why timing matters less than the total. If one scoop closes the gap in your day, the tub lasts twice as long.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two scoops also add about 240 calories, which is worth knowing on a cut. A single 120-calorie scoop with 25 g of protein is a reasonable fit for a calorie-controlled diet, while a two-scoop shake made with milk can reach 400 calories or more. GNC's Amazon listing says one or two scoops in 8 oz of water or your favorite drink, so the one-scoop option is on the label, not a workaround.
          </p>
        </div>

