        <div id="testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Testing: What Informed Choice Covers
          </h2>

          <p>
            GNC says the New Wheybolic is Informed Choice certified and Informed Protein certified, and the Amazon listing repeats the Informed Choice claim, adding that it is regularly tested for more than 285 banned substances in sport. Both programs are run by LGC, the UK anti-doping lab. GNC's launch announcement also says every batch is tested for banned substances, which it presents as support for athletes in drug-tested sports.
          </p>

          <p>
            We could not confirm a current New Wheybolic entry in the Informed Choice database, because the registry blocked automated access when we checked. Search results did show older Informed Choice listings for GNC products, including Wheybolic Ripped (certified in 2017) and the Wheybolic 40 ready-to-drink shake (2018), so GNC has used the program for years. If certification matters to you, search the registry yourself for the exact product and flavor.
          </p>

          <p>
            Informed Choice and Informed Sport are not the same thing. In LGC's description, Informed Choice tests regular blind samples of a product bought at retail, while Informed Sport tests every batch before it is released and lists batch numbers. Most athletes are fine with either, but some teams and governing bodies name a specific program. We found no NSF Certified for Sport listing for Wheybolic, so if your league requires NSF, look at our <a href="/best-nsf-certified-protein-powder/">NSF certified protein powder</a> picks instead.
          </p>

          <p>
            Informed Protein is the more interesting badge for ordinary buyers. It checks that the protein on the label is really there and screens for amino spiking, the old trick of padding the protein figure with cheap free amino acids like glycine. Wheybolic adds free L-leucine, so independent verification that the 25 g is whey protein rather than added aminos is a useful safeguard.
          </p>

          <p>
            GNC also calls Wheybolic America's most clinically proven protein powder for strength, and older listings claimed a 30% strength increase in 8 weeks. We could not review the studies behind those claims, so we cannot tell you how they were designed or what Wheybolic was compared against. Whey plus resistance training reliably builds strength; that is a property of good whey in general. Our <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">protein powder safety guide</a> covers what testing claims can and cannot tell you.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="flavors" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Flavors and What Buyers Can Expect
          </h2>

          <p>
            On Amazon the New Wheybolic was listed in Chocolate Fudge, Classic Vanilla, Strawberries and Cream, Cookies and Cream and Banana Milkshake at the time of writing. GNC's launch announcement listed a longer line-up for its stores: Chocolate, Vanilla, Cookies 'n Cream, Strawberry, Fruity Cereal, Banana, Natural Chocolate, Natural Vanilla and Peanut Butter. Availability varies by size, so not every flavor comes in both tubs.
          </p>

          <p>
            The old formula had a much longer and stranger flavor list, including Apple Crisp, Chocolate Mint, Coconut Caramel, Fruity Crisps, Peppermint Hot Cocoa, Salted Caramel Apple and some Girl Scout cookie flavors made under license. Many of those are seasonal or limited runs and are now hard to find. If you have a favorite old flavor, the leftover old-formula tubs on Amazon may be the last chance, at the old 20 g per scoop.
          </p>

          <p>
            The new formula is too recent for much independent taste feedback, and we found little beyond GNC's own claims. GNC says it improved taste, texture and mixability and that the powder now works well in water or milk. A more protein-dense scoop with less filler often mixes thinner, so expect a lighter shake than the old version, especially in water. We will update this section as more buyer reviews of the 2026 tubs appear.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two flavor choices have practical consequences. Chocolate proteins of any brand tend to carry more lead and cadmium than vanilla, because cocoa naturally contains them, so vanilla is the cautious daily choice. And the Natural Chocolate and Natural Vanilla versions that GNC lists in stores are the ones to ask about if you want to avoid artificial flavors, though you should still read the sweetener line on the tub.
          </p>

          <p>
            If flavor is your main reason for buying, compare before you commit to a 26-serving tub. Buy a 13-serving tub of the flavor you want to try, or look at our <a href="/best-tasting-protein-powder/">best tasting protein powder</a> guide, which ranks dessert-style flavors across brands. Mixing a scoop with milk instead of water adds about 8 g of protein and makes most whey taste richer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="lineup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Wheybolic Original, Ripped or Alpha 2.0: Which to Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            GNC now sells three powders under the Wheybolic name, and they are not interchangeable. Wheybolic Original, the New Wheybolic, is the plain whey covered above. Wheybolic Ripped is a cutting formula with stimulants. Wheybolic Alpha 2.0 is a whey isolate with collagen and a fava bean peptide. For most people the right answer is the plain one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wheybolic Ripped was updated in October 2026, according to Stack3d. The new version lists 50 g of protein per two scoops from whey isolate and concentrate, about 290 calories, and adds 3 g of CLA, 200 mg of caffeine, capsicum, piperine and niacin. That caffeine matters: two scoops equal about two cups of coffee, so it is not a bedtime shake or something to stack with a pre-workout. Our <a href="/best-fat-burning-protein-powder/">fat burning protein powder</a> guide explains why the extras add little.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wheybolic Alpha 2.0 is the premium option. Amazon describes it as a 4-in-1 formula of whey isolate, collagen peptides, PeptiStrong fava bean peptide and L-leucine, taken as two scoops. At $124.99 for 26 servings and $69.99 for 13 on Amazon, it costs more than the New Wheybolic. The earlier Alpha label counted about 5 g of collagen in its 50 g of protein, and collagen lacks the amino acid profile of whey, so check the protein source before treating it as a 50 g whey shake.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our pick is Wheybolic Original, the New Wheybolic, for nearly everyone. It has the cleanest label of the three, no caffeine, the most protein from whey, and the lowest price. Choose Ripped only if you want caffeine in your shake and have checked that 200 mg fits your day. Choose Alpha 2.0 only if you specifically want collagen and the fava peptide, and are willing to pay a premium for them.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whichever you buy, confirm the version on the tub. Old 40 g Wheybolic, the 50 g New Wheybolic, Ripped and Alpha 2.0 can sit on the same shelf in similar packaging. The words New Wheybolic or Wheybolic Original, the 25 g per scoop claim and the Informed Choice logo identify the reformulated plain whey.
          </p>
        </div>

