        <div id="who" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. Who Should Buy Wheybolic, and Who Should Skip It
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy New Wheybolic if you shop at GNC, can catch a promotion, and want a dense, certified whey with 25 g of protein per scoop. It is a better product than the old 40 g formula and a reasonable choice for tested athletes who are comfortable with Informed Choice, provided you check the registry. Use one scoop unless you are a large lifter or need 50 g to hit your daily target.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it on Amazon at full price. At about $2.31 per 25 g it costs more than a pure hydrolyzed isolate and far more than Gold Standard. Skip it, too, if you are lactose-sensitive, since the blend likely contains some concentrate, or if you avoid sucralose and acesulfame potassium and cannot confirm the sweeteners on your tub. A pure, stevia-sweetened isolate is the safer choice for both.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip Wheybolic Ripped if you are sensitive to caffeine, drink coffee and a pre-workout, or train in the evening. Its 200 mg of caffeine per two-scoop serving is a full dose. Skip Alpha 2.0 if your goal is simply more protein, because the collagen and fava peptide add cost without adding the complete amino acids that make whey worth buying.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you have kidney disease, are pregnant or breastfeeding, have a milk allergy or take medication, talk to your doctor or a registered dietitian before adding a high-protein supplement, especially at 50 g a serving. Healthy adults generally tolerate whey well at normal doses. This review covers labels, testing claims and value. It is not medical advice.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
